# Core envelope verification

Download this `.livemd` file, open it in [Livebook](https://livebook.dev), and choose
**Open → Import from file**, then **Run all**. Use Elixir 1.18 or later and OTP 27 or later
with access to public Hex. No secrets or external services are needed.

Build a contract-major 1 grant and holder proof for reading a record, verify their request
binding, and refuse changed bytes, disallowed arguments, and stale proofs.

The host supplies trusted issuer keys and request context. Verification produces facts, not
execution authority; revocation, replay reservation, and the final decision belong to the host.
Demonstration keys stay in memory. This notebook starts no processes and creates no files.

[Open the original 1.0.0 release notebook](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fbaselabs%2Fbounded_authority_protocol%2Fblob%2Fv1.0.0%2Fdocs%2Flivebooks%2Fbap-walkthrough.livemd).
That immutable release has the earlier walkthrough; import this file for the steps below.

## Setup

Install the exact published package. Fixed evaluation times keep the results reproducible;
only safe semantic values are persisted, never keys, signatures, tokens, or nonces.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:bounded_authority_protocol, "== 1.0.0"}])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:ok
```

## Generate issuer and holder keys

Generate two independent Ed25519 keys. The grant identifies its holder by the raw RFC 7638
thumbprint, not by private key material.

```elixir
{issuer_public, issuer_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
{holder_public, holder_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)

{:ok, holder_thumbprint} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.Jwk.public_key_thumbprint_raw(holder_public, %{})

{byte_size(issuer_public), byte_size(holder_thumbprint)}
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{32, 32}
```

## Build and sign the grant

The issuer permits one operation under conjunctive selectors. Sign the exact JWS input with
`:crypto`; the protocol producer receives only the resulting signature.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{Grant, Operation}

grant = %Grant{
  key_id: "issuer-1",
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  grant_id: "grant-2026-001",
  audiences: ["https://service.example"],
  issued_at: 1_800_000_000,
  not_before: 1_800_000_000,
  expires_at: 1_800_003_600,
  holder_thumbprint: holder_thumbprint,
  operations: [
    %Operation{
      name: "read_record",
      selectors: [
        {:equals, ["record", "region"], {:string, "us-east"}},
        {:one_of, ["record", "tier"], [{:string, "gold"}, {:string, "platinum"}]},
        :all
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{:ok, signing_input} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.grant_signing_input(grant, %{})
issuer_signature = :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, signing_input.message, [issuer_private, :ed25519])

{:ok, grant_compact} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.assemble_compact(signing_input, issuer_signature)

:grant_signed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:grant_signed
```

## Build and sign the holder proof

Bind the exact grant compact bytes, method, target, invocation, operation, and tagged arguments.
The holder signs this input with the key the grant names.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.Proof

now = 1_800_001_000

proof = %Proof{
  holder_public_key: holder_public,
  proof_id: "proof-2026-001",
  method: "GET",
  target_uri: "https://service.example/records/1",
  issued_at: now,
  nonce: nil,
  invocation_id: "7d444840-9dc0-11ed-a8fc-0242ac120002",
  operation: "read_record",
  grant_compact: grant_compact,
  cast_arguments:
    {:object,
     [
       {"record", {:object, [{"tier", {:string, "gold"}}, {"region", {:string, "us-east"}}]}}
     ]}
}

{:ok, proof_input} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.proof_signing_input(proof, %{})
holder_signature = :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, proof_input.message, [holder_private, :ed25519])
{:ok, proof_compact} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.assemble_compact(proof_input, holder_signature)

:proof_signed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:proof_signed
```

## Verify the grant

Supply an already-trusted issuer key, expected audience, and evaluation time. Project only safe
identifiers and the explicit non-authorization marker from the returned facts.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{TrustedIssuer, ExpectedGrant}

trusted = %TrustedIssuer{key_id: "issuer-1", public_key: issuer_public}

expected_grant = %ExpectedGrant{
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  audience: "https://service.example",
  evaluation_time: now,
  clock_skew: 60,
  bounds: %{}
}

{:ok, grant_facts} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.verify_grant(grant_compact, trusted, expected_grant)

Map.take(grant_facts, [:issuer, :grant_id, :authorization])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{issuer: "https://issuer.example", grant_id: "grant-2026-001", authorization: :not_evaluated}
```

## Verify the envelope

Derive the expected request from the host request boundary. Envelope verification rechecks the
grant, holder signature, request binding, freshness, and every selector.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{Credentials, ExpectedRequest}

credentials = %Credentials{grant: grant_compact, proof: proof_compact}

expected_request = %ExpectedRequest{
  trusted_issuer: trusted,
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  audience: "https://service.example",
  method: "GET",
  target_uri: "https://service.example/records/1",
  invocation_id: "7d444840-9dc0-11ed-a8fc-0242ac120002",
  operation: "read_record",
  cast_arguments:
    {:object,
     [
       {"record", {:object, [{"tier", {:string, "gold"}}, {"region", {:string, "us-east"}}]}}
     ]},
  evaluation_time: now,
  clock_skew: 60,
  proof_max_age: 300,
  nonce: :not_required,
  bounds: %{}
}

{:ok, envelope_facts} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.check_envelope(credentials, expected_request)

Map.take(envelope_facts, [:operation, :authorization])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{operation: "read_record", authorization: :not_evaluated}
```

## Refuse a changed signature

Flip a bit in the decoded signature and re-encode it canonically. The grant fails even though
its header and payload are unchanged.

```elixir
[header, payload, encoded_signature] = String.split(grant_compact, ".")
<<first, rest::binary>> = Base.url_decode64!(encoded_signature, padding: false)
changed_signature = Base.url_encode64(<<Bitwise.bxor(first, 1), rest::binary>>, padding: false)
tampered = Enum.join([header, payload, changed_signature], ".")

{:error, :invalid} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.check_envelope(%{credentials | grant: tampered}, expected_request)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:error, :invalid}
```

## Refuse changed request arguments

A valid proof cannot be reused for a different request body. The signed request digest must
match the host-derived tagged arguments.

```elixir
wrong_arguments =
  {:object,
   [{"record", {:object, [{"tier", {:string, "bronze"}}, {"region", {:string, "us-east"}}]}}]}

{:error, :invalid} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.check_envelope(credentials, %{
    expected_request
    | cast_arguments: wrong_arguments
  })
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:error, :invalid}
```

## Refuse a matching proof outside the selectors

Sign a fresh proof for the disallowed arguments so its request binding is correct. This
refusal isolates selector enforcement from the previous digest mismatch.

```elixir
{:ok, disallowed_input} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.proof_signing_input(%{proof | cast_arguments: wrong_arguments}, %{})

disallowed_signature =
  :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, disallowed_input.message, [holder_private, :ed25519])

{:ok, disallowed_proof} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.assemble_compact(disallowed_input, disallowed_signature)

{:error, :invalid} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.check_envelope(%{credentials | proof: disallowed_proof}, %{
    expected_request
    | cast_arguments: wrong_arguments
  })
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:error, :invalid}
```

## Refuse a stale proof

The proof is too old after its maximum age plus skew, while the grant is still live. The closed
error exposes no details to the caller.

```elixir
{:error, :invalid} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.check_envelope(credentials, %{
    expected_request
    | evaluation_time: now + 361
  })
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:error, :invalid}
```

Continue with [service integration](service-integration.livemd) for host-owned trust rotation
and replay retention, or [troubleshooting](../guides/troubleshooting.md) for diagnosing refusals.
